.Dd April 16, 2026
.Dt AMDPOWER 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm amdpower
.Nd AMD CPU power sensor driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "device amdpower"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver exposes average CPU package and per-physical-core power consumption
for AMD Family 17h
.Pq Zen, Zen+, Zen2 ,
Family 19h
.Pq Zen3, Zen4 ,
and Family 1Ah
.Pq Zen5
processors via
.Xr sysctl 8 .
.Pp
To load the driver as a module at boot time, add the following line to
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
amdpower_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
Power is measured using the RAPL
.Pq Running Average Power Limit
energy counters documented in the AMD Processor Programming Reference
.Pq PPR :
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Li 0xC0010299
.Sy RAPL_PWR_UNIT :
energy, power, and time unit exponents.
.It Li 0xC001029A
.Sy CORE_ENERGY_STATUS :
per-physical-core energy accumulator
.Pq 32-bit, wrapping .
Reading this MSR on logical CPU
.Va N
returns the accumulated energy of the physical core that CPU
.Va N
belongs to.
When SMT is active, both logical threads of a physical core return the
same counter.
.It Li 0xC001029B
.Sy PKG_ENERGY_STATUS :
package
.Pq socket
energy accumulator.
The counter is 32-bit on Family 17h and 19h, and 64-bit on Family 1Ah.
.El
.Pp
The driver identifies physical cores at attach time using CPUID leaf
.Li 0x8000001E
to determine the number of SMT threads per core, then reads each
physical core's energy counter from the first logical CPU of that core.
.Pp
Power is computed as the energy delta between two snapshots divided by the
elapsed time.
Snapshots are taken every
.Va interval_ms
milliseconds via a callout.
.Sh HARDWARE
The
.Nm
driver supports AMD processors based on the following microarchitectures:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
AMD Family 17h: Zen
.Pq Summit Ridge, Naples ,
Zen+
.Pq Pinnacle Ridge, Picasso ,
Zen2
.Pq Matisse, Rome, Castle Peak, Renoir
.It
AMD Family 19h: Zen3
.Pq Vermeer, Milan, Cezanne ,
Zen3+
.Pq Rembrandt ,
Zen4
.Pq Raphael, Genoa, Phoenix
.It
AMD Family 1Ah: Zen5
.Pq Granite Ridge, Turin, Strix
.El
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following
.Xr sysctl 8
variables are provided under
.Va dev.amdpower.<unit> :
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va pkg_power
Read-only.
Average package power in milliwatts over the last
.Va interval_ms
milliseconds.
The package reading covers the entire processor package as defined by AMD.
.It Va core0_power , core1_power , No ...
Read-only.
Average power in milliwatts for each physical core over the last
.Va interval_ms
milliseconds.
The number of
.Va core<N>_power
variables matches the number of physical cores detected at attach time.
The sysctl description includes the logical CPU number used to read each
core's energy MSR.
.It Va interval_ms
Read-write.
Measurement interval in milliseconds.
Default is 250.
Shorter values increase responsiveness; longer values reduce noise.
.El
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
Power readings are averages over the preceding
.Va interval_ms
milliseconds and are updated by a periodic callout, not on each
.Xr sysctl 8
read.
The first reading after driver attach is always 0.
.Pp
The 32-bit per-core energy counter
.Pq and the 32-bit package counter on Family 17h and 19h
wraps; wrap is handled transparently by unsigned 32-bit subtraction of
consecutive snapshots.
At an energy unit shift of 16 and 300 W sustained draw, a 32-bit counter
wraps in approximately 218 seconds, well within any realistic polling
interval.
The 64-bit package counter on Family 1Ah does not wrap in practice.
.Pp
Reading per-core energy MSRs requires briefly pinning the polling thread to
each target CPU via
.Fn sched_bind .
This introduces minor scheduling perturbation proportional to the number of
physical cores and the measurement frequency.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr amdtemp 4 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
